 epiphytic orchid which is a hybrid of Bulbophyllum longissimum and Bulbophyllum rothschildianum. The plant produces short, angled, egg-shaped green stems called pseudobulbs, which are adapted to store water and nutrients. The pseudobulbs arise from a creeping, rooting stem, and each are topped with a single, green, oblong to lance-shaped, leaf. Flowers are produced from autumn to spring, in clusters at the end of thin stems and are pale pink with darker pink fine stripes. The upper sepal is somewhat hairy, and pale green with darker stripes, while the lower sepals are up to 30cm long.  The hinged lip, an adapted petal evolved to attract pollinators, moves in the slightest breeze, is tongue-shaped and pink. The flowers are scented and appear from autumn to spring","image":"BulbophyllumElizabethAnnBucklebury.jpg","commonName":"bulbophyllum Elizabeth Ann gx 'Bucklerberry'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":165100,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Bulbophyllum</em> Elizabeth Ann gx 'Buckleberry'","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":29336,"botanicalName":"<em>Cirrhopetalum</em> <em>medusae</em>","entityDescription":"A fast-growing, branching orchid, with a creeping stem found growing on the bark of trunks and large tree branches. It prefers warm to hot growing conditions, partial shade and high humidity such as is found in wet, tropical lowland forests and mangroves.  It produces swollen stems called pseudobulbs which store water and nutrients, each carrying a single leaf at the tip. Clusters of up to 50 white, spidery flowers, with an unpleasant smell, are produced  at the top of a flower stem up to 20cm long. These are produced on a newly forming pseudobulb in autumn and winter","image":"Bulbophyllummedusae.jpg","commonName":"Medusa's head orchid","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":88527,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Bulbophyllum</em> <em>medusae</em>","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":fals